E4 — STALE RESPONSE / OUT-OF-ORDER RACE

Code trace:
  refresh(): fires Promise.all([connections, feed]) — both awaited
    No request cancellation. If user triggers refresh twice rapidly:
    - First refresh starts
    - Second refresh starts (isLoading already true, but no guard prevents second call)
    - First completes → sets state
    - Second completes → overwrites with potentially stale data
    VERDICT: DOWNGRADE — LOW_IMPACT_TRANSIENT_STATE
    Reason: No request cancellation, but both requests fetch fresh server truth.
    The "stale" response is from seconds ago, not a fundamentally different generation.
    In practice this causes at most a brief flicker, not data corruption.

  loadNextFeedPage(): guarded by loadingMore flag
    VERDICT: PASS — in-flight guard prevents concurrent Load More

  Like: guarded by likingId
    VERDICT: PASS — in-flight guard

E5 — PAGINATION RESILIENCE

Code trace:
  loadNextFeedPage():
    - If !hasMore || !nextCursor || loadingMore → early return (no-op)
    - On failure: loadingMore=false, error set, toast shown
    - Existing feed NOT destroyed on failure (only appends on success)
    - Retry: user clicks Load More again → uses same cursor (truthful)
    VERDICT: PASS

  Refresh during Load More:
    - refresh() replaces entire feed (does not check loadingMore)
    - If Load More is in-flight and refresh completes first:
      Load More's append will use the OLD nextCursor (from before refresh)
      → may append stale/duplicate items
    VERDICT: DOWNGRADE — LOW_IMPACT_TRANSIENT_STATE
    Reason: refresh() does not cancel in-flight loadNextFeedPage. But the store's
    dedup (existingIds filter) prevents duplicates. At worst, some items may be
    appended that were already in the refreshed feed (filtered by dedup → no dup).

E6 — SESSION/AUTH FAILURE

Code trace:
  All social API calls use fetch/csrfFetch without explicit 401 handling
  If session expires mid-session:
    - GET requests return 401 → response.ok=false → error pushed to errors[]
    - Mutations return 401 → response.ok=false → throw Error("Failed (401)")
    - No redirect to login page from social stores
    - UI shows error toast but does not prompt re-authentication
  
  VERDICT: DOWNGRADE — RECOVERY_REQUIRES_RELOAD
    Reason: 401 is handled as a generic error, not as auth-expired. User sees
    error toast but must manually reload to trigger auth redirect.
    No false success — mutations throw on 401. Just not user-friendly recovery.

E7 — ERROR MESSAGE SAFETY

Code trace:
  src/lib/errors.ts withErrorHandler:
    - Known AppError → returns { error: { code, message, traceId, details } }
    - Unknown error → returns { error: { code: 'INTERNAL_ERROR', message: 'An unexpected error occurred. Please retry.', details: { name: err.name } } }
    - console.error logs full error server-side (not exposed to client)
  
  Error response shape: { error: { code, message, traceId, details } }
    - code: UPPERCASE error code (e.g., 'AUTHENTICATION_REQUIRED', 'VALIDATION_ERROR')
    - message: Human-readable, generic (e.g., 'Authentication required', 'Invalid cursor format')
    - traceId: UUID for server-side correlation
    - details: { name: err.name } for INTERNAL_ERROR — exposes Error.name only
  
  Client-side error handling:
    - social-store.ts: body?.error || `Failed (${res.status})` — extracts error message
    - friends-screen.tsx: (e as Error).message — shows thrown Error message
    - social-screen.tsx: toast description uses Error.message
  
  Check for sensitive leakage:
    - No SQL/Prisma details in error responses (generic INTERNAL_ERROR)
    - No stack traces (console.error only, not in response)
    - No session tokens or CSRF tokens
    - No raw block metadata (S4B canUnblock projection only)
    - No phone information (S4B removed from search response)
    - Error.name exposed in details for INTERNAL_ERROR — could leak 'PrismaClientKnownRequestError'
  
  VERDICT: DOWNGRADE — UX_WEAKNESS
    Reason: Error.name in details could leak 'PrismaClientKnownRequestError' for
    unhandled Prisma errors. But social routes catch P2002 explicitly and
    withTransaction wraps in TransactionConflictError. In practice, INTERNAL_ERROR
    is rare and Error.name is a minor diagnostic leak, not a security issue.

